      "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0",
      "artist": "RichardMcCoy",
      "description": "The City Power House, which is also known as the Pump House, Columbus Power House, Holly Works, and the Senior Center, was designed by architect Harrison Albright and completed in 1903. The building stands on the banks of the East Fork of the White River at the foot of Second Street in Columbus, IN, Indiana. From 1903 to 1951 it pumped water from the river, which was then filtered and used domestically; it also produced the electricity for the city's street lighting, hence the name \"Power House.\" The city sold the property in 1952. It later became a Shell gas station and with a renovation that was completed in 1974 became the \"Senior Center.\"",
      "dateCreated": "Taken on 17 June 2013, 13:39:33",
